E. Coli (es@ritz.mordor.com) wrote:
:    The *actual* setup was this:

:    You have a box, a cat, and a vial of poison.  Cat and poison are in the 
: box.  If you break the vial which is inside the box and release the 
: poison, at any given time you cannot predict whether or not the cat is 
: dead, but you know that sometime between the vial breaking and the 
: removal of the dead cat, the cat dies.

:    This is basically the idea behind quantum mechanics and the Heisenberg 
: uncertainty principle.  You can not predict the location of an electron 
: at any given time (for example), but you can say that an electron has 
: been at a certain place.  This continues with the idea of you can't know 
: time while knowing space (time-space is a very touchy subject), and vice 
: versa.

:     Pick up an elementary quantum theory book and take a good Sunday 
: reading of it.

Well, I can't resist.  Mainly because someone who is a real expert will
pounce on me and I guess I need to learn some of this myself.

The Schroedinger's Cat "Gedanken" Experiment has nothing to do with 
Heisenberg's famed 'Uncertainty Principle". It deals with the superposition
of quantum states in that the cat is BOTH alive AND dead at the same point
in space time.

As far as the PoC (Principle of Uncertainty :-) goes, you can measure the
location of an electron as accurately as you want... you just lose your
ability to measure it's momentum.  (This applies to any two conjugate
variables.  Time and Energy are another set.)

